 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "In September twenty twenty-five, a street in Bangkok fell into a hole fifty meters deep.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It swallowed power poles, cars and the road right outside a hospital.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Nobody was hurt. But how does a solid street just vanish?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Bangkok is built on soft clay and sand, soaked with water.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Right next to that street, crews were digging a new subway station.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Deep down, water and wet sand started leaking into the dig.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Sand mixed with water is called running sand. It flows like a liquid.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So hour after hour, soil drained out from under the road.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "On top, the asphalt still looked fine. It was hiding an empty space.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then a water main under the street broke, and added even more water.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The thin crust of road gave way, all at once.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Crews poured concrete and sand into the hole, and promised the road back in two weeks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why subway builders watch the groundwater day and night.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Because the scariest holes start where you can't see them.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
